<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rudrabhatta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rudrabhatta was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> period, patronised by a minister of the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a>. His Jagannatha Vijaya retells the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and is one of the earliest Vaishnava works in Kannada literature. It is written in the champu style, mixing verse and prose.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rudrabhatta was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> and a Smartha, a follower of monistic philosophy, who wrote at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire</a>, who reigned from 1173 to 1220. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> scholar Narasimhacharya, who records that a minister of the king was also his patron, dates his Jagannatha Vijaya to about 1180 from inscriptions of the reign. The critic Mukherjee suggests that, after a century of literary revolution led by the Veerashaiva poets, the tolerant atmosphere the king created may have encouraged a Vaishnava writer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Jagannatha Vijaya is based on the Sanskrit Vishnu Purana. This kavya, or narrative poem, follows the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> up to his fight with the demon Banasura. In it Rudrabhatta presents Hari (Vishnu), Hara (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> as one composite supreme deity, Parabrahma, who takes the form of Krishna.</p><p>The scholar Sreekantaiyya assigns to him, on internal evidence and on references by the later poet Salva in his Rasaratnakara of about 1550, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> work Rasakalika. He holds that the Sringaratilaka, a Sanskrit book on love and aesthetics in poetry by an author of the same name, is not his; according to Dalal its author lived in the 10th century and was also called Rudratta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>According to Dalal, Rudrabhatta influenced the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poets Kumaravyasa and Lakshmisha and the Haridasa composers Purandaradasa and Kanakadasa. L. S. Sheshagiri Rao regards him as a poet for the learned classes, while Shiva Kumar sees an ancient form with a more medieval content, which makes him a poet of transition. Shiva Prakash and Dalal consider him skilled in both the marga, or mainstream, and the desi, or native, styles. Sankaranarayanan holds that the Rasakalika shaped Indian aesthetics, serving as a source for Vidyanatha's Prataparudriya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rudrabhatta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The period was also rich in Kannada literature: Harihara and Raghavanka pioneered new metres and themes, Janna and <b>Rudrabhatta</b> wrote major works on Jain and Vaishnava subjects, and Kesiraja’s Shabdamanidarpana of 1260 became the classic grammar of Old Kannada.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
